WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.500
A mouse has about a thousand odorant-receptor genes.

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.960
It reads an odour from a pattern across its receptors.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.900
It also has dozens of bitter-receptor genes.

4
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:15.940
Yet bitter is one taste.

5
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:17.820
Why the difference?

6
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.310
Start with one receptor cell.

7
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:24.090
Its output is its activity: how strongly it is active.

8
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:29.730
Two different molecules arrive, each at a concentration that activates it equally.

9
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:32.923
Can this cell tell which molecule it met?

10
00:00:38.866 --> 00:00:45.086
No. A receptor cell reports how strongly it is active, not which molecule activated

11
00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:46.086
it.

12
00:00:46.299 --> 00:00:48.279
Vision calls this univariance.

13
00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:52.959
A cone cannot tell a dim light near its peak from a brighter one far from it.

14
00:00:53.849 --> 00:00:58.709
To tell molecules apart when their concentrations vary, a sense needs more than one

15
00:00:58.709 --> 00:01:03.809
line. A line is a set of cells whose activity reaches the brain as one channel.

16
00:01:04.783 --> 00:01:09.623
In smell, a line is all the neurons with one receptor, and the glomeruli they

17
00:01:09.623 --> 00:01:10.623
converge on.

18
00:01:11.266 --> 00:01:16.206
In taste, a line is one kind of receptor cell: the sweet cells, the bitter cells,

19
00:01:16.386 --> 00:01:17.386
and so on.

20
00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:22.739
A pattern is the relative strength of activity across lines, not just which lines

21
00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:23.739
are on.

22
00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:29.169
The rule: a sense can tell two stimuli apart only if their patterns across separate

23
00:01:29.169 --> 00:01:30.169
lines differ.

24
00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:34.419
Smell keeps about a thousand receptor types on separate lines.

25
00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:37.879
So an odour is read as a pattern across many lines.

26
00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:41.939
Taste pools its receptors into a few kinds of cell.

27
00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:45.739
So a taste is read mainly from which kind of cell is active.

28
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:51.963
A line whose activity always means one quality, whatever turned it on, is called a

29
00:01:51.963 --> 00:01:52.963
labeled line.

30
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:54.943
Keep the scope in view.

31
00:01:55.823 --> 00:01:59.883
The rule is about the periphery, mostly in mice, and it ignores spike timing.

32
00:02:00.966 --> 00:02:03.866
Gene count alone does not set the number of lines.

33
00:02:04.786 --> 00:02:08.646
What matters is whether receptor identity is kept separate or pooled.
